    "approach": "Keep Eureka's technical name and job distinct, use the exact parent logo master in a separate endorsement position, and inherit the M:OS palette, typography, evidence discipline and documentation conventions.",
    "workflowFile": "docs/brand-application.md",
    "steps": [
      {
        "phase": "brief",
        "action": "Record the README reader, family relationship, job, evidence and selected brand segments."
      },
      {
        "phase": "produce",
        "action": "Use the canonical parent lockup and family/documentation rules while preserving exact CLI, MCP and capability identifiers."
      },
      {
        "phase": "review",
        "action": "Check repository links and hierarchy, preserve product facts and maturity labels, and verify the logo and asset provenance."
      },
      {
        "phase": "return-learning",
        "action": "Record unresolved family identity and product integration facts for follow-up in the Eureka repository."
      }
    ]
  },
  "why": {
    "purpose": "Make Eureka legible as a distinct M:OS adjunct service and make its repository easy to understand and use.",
    "evidence": "Owner request in this conversation: redesign using the M:OS brand book and identify Mindrian Eureka as an adjunct service for M:OS; repository README and capability catalog provide current technical scope.",
    "success": "The README uses an exact parent lockup as endorsement, states Eureka's distinct job and boundary, and guides a reader to method, capabilities, setup and readiness without overstating integration."
  },
  "jtbd": {
    "when": "a MindrianOS user or developer arrives at the Eureka repository to understand how it helps explore uncertainty",
    "want": "see its role in the M:OS family, understand what work it supports, and find the right CLI or MCP entrypoint",
    "so": "assess fit and make a grounded next move without confusing opportunity exploration with a solved problem or assuming host behavior that is not verified",
    "statement": "When a MindrianOS user or developer arrives at the Eureka repository to understand how it helps explore uncertainty, I want to see its role in the M:OS family, understand what work it supports, and find the right CLI or MCP entrypoint, so I can assess fit and make a grounded next move without confusing opportunity exploration with a solved problem or assuming host behavior that is not verified."
